# config/auth.conf.example - authentication identities (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.6.2)
#
# Copy to config/auth.conf, edit, and `chmod 600` it.  scoursh REFUSES to read
# this file at any other mode (E073): every value in it is a credential.
#
# One record per (target, identity).  The `id` is `<target-id>.<label>`, where
# `<target-id>` MUST name a target your config/scope.conf defines (E080) and
# `<label>` is yours to choose.  Two labelled identities on one target is what
# the cross-user access-control checks need - they work by asking for identity
# B's object as identity A, so with one identity they cannot run at all.
#
# CREDENTIALS: prefer `secret-file`, an ABSOLUTE path to a mode-600 file holding
# the credential, over the inline `token` / `password` / `client-secret` keys.
# The inline keys work, but they put the credential in this file, and this file
# is the one somebody pastes into a ticket.  A secret-file is read as its FIRST
# LINE, so a trailing newline from your editor is not part of the credential.
# scoursh never passes a credential as a command-line argument and never writes
# one to disk in the clear (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 9).
#
# Which keys each `mode` requires is checked as E074 when the file loads, so a
# missing key is reported here rather than at the first request to your target.
#
# Nothing below is a default and no host named here exists: every value is an
# RFC 2606 reserved example domain or a placeholder path.  scoursh ships no
# scannable target of its own (docs/DESIGN.md §1).

# --- a static bearer token -------------------------------------------------
# Sends `Authorization: Bearer <credential>`.  `header-name` moves it to
# another header if your API wants one; the `Bearer ` prefix stays.
id: staging.a
mode: bearer
secret-file: /etc/scoursh/staging-a.token
notes: A long-lived token you already hold. scoursh performs no login for this
  mode and sends no request to acquire the session.

# --- a raw API key ---------------------------------------------------------
# Sends the credential VERBATIM under `header-name` - no `Bearer ` prefix,
# because an API key is not a bearer token and prefixing it would send a
# credential shape the target never issued.  `header-name` defaults to
# `Authorization` if you omit it.
id: staging.b
mode: api-key
secret-file: /etc/scoursh/staging-b.key
header-name: X-API-Key
notes: The second identity on this target. Two identities is what the
  cross-user access-control checks need.

# --- form login ------------------------------------------------------------
# POSTs the credentials to `login-path` (relative to the target's base-url) and
# keeps whatever the exchange leaves behind: a Set-Cookie jar, a token in the
# response body, or both.
#
# The request BODY SHAPE is not part of this schema, so scoursh tries at most
# three, in order, and stops at the first that yields a session:
#   1. application/x-www-form-urlencoded  username=<u>&password=<p>
#   2. application/json                   {"email":<u>,"password":<p>}
#   3. application/json                   {"username":<u>,"password":<p>}
# The winning shape is remembered, so a re-authentication replays it directly
# and never probes again.  If your target has an account lockout policy, be
# aware that a wholly failed login costs three attempts, once, per identity.
id: app.a
mode: form
username: scan-test-a@example.com
secret-file: /etc/scoursh/app-a.password
login-path: /api/login

# --- OAuth2 / OIDC, resource-owner password grant (RFC 6749 §4.3) ----------
# `token-url` is a full URL and is scope-gated like every other request: put
# its host in config/scope.conf, or the run refuses to contact it.
# `client-secret` is optional here (a public client has none); if you set both
# it and `secret-file`, the secret-file holds the USER's password.
id: api.a
mode: oauth2-password
token-url: https://idp.example.com/oauth2/token
username: scan-test-a@example.com
secret-file: /etc/scoursh/api-a.password
client-id: scoursh-scan
scope: openid profile

# --- OAuth2, client-credentials grant (RFC 6749 §4.4) ---------------------
# No user; the client authenticates as itself. `secret-file` holds the CLIENT
# secret for this mode. Client credentials are sent as body parameters rather
# than HTTP Basic; RFC 6749 §2.3.1 permits both.
id: api.svc
mode: oauth2-client
token-url: https://idp.example.com/oauth2/token
client-id: scoursh-scan
secret-file: /etc/scoursh/api-client.secret
scope: api.read

# --- an identity-provider SRP pool (Cognito-style) -------------------------
# scoursh does NOT compute the SRP handshake: docs/DESIGN.md §7.0 permits
# either computing it or accepting a pre-obtained token, and this build accepts
# the token - a pure-shell SRP-6a implementation is unverifiable crypto in a
# language with no way to test it. Obtain the token with your provider's own
# CLI or SDK, put it in a 600 file, and point `secret-file` at it. The run
# records that the SRP exchange was not performed, so nobody reads `mode: srp`
# as evidence that your provider's SRP implementation was exercised.
id: api.pooled
mode: srp
username: scan-test-a@example.com
pool-id: replace-with-your-pool-id
secret-file: /etc/scoursh/api-pooled.jwt

# --- a credential obtained entirely out of band ----------------------------
# Mechanically the same as api-key, recorded differently on purpose: nothing in
# the run checked that this session was still valid, and a reader of the report
# needs to know that was nobody's job.
id: api.external
mode: external
secret-file: /etc/scoursh/api-external.token
notes: Refreshed by something outside scoursh. If it expires mid-run, the
  authenticated checks are marked skipped with that reason - never run
  unauthenticated and reported as clean.
